Z||000008||pherder||06-28-2001||02:31 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>I've determined that there are stock GM axles that will work for putting a 440T4/4T60 trans in a Fiero. 1987 Pontiac 6000, no ABS, light duty brakes. These axles fit perfectly with no mods. A search should reveal the rest of the answers.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Rockcrawl,<P><B>THANK YOU, THANK YOU, THANK YOU!!!</B><P>Exactly the info I have been searching for!!!<P>Now, another question ...<P>I am out @ the CV Unlimited web site looking @ the different half-shafts listed for the Pontiac 6000 ... which ones are the ones needed for the Fiero swap?<P> <A HREF="http://www.cvunlimited.com/Catalog2.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cvunlimited.com/Catalog2.htm</A> <P>1984-89 4 SPD. A/T W/.785" DIA. THREADS       5257 5258     <P>1984-91 4 SPD. A/T W/.940" DIA. THREADS W/O ABS     5255 5256     <BR> <BR>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000009||SteveJ||06-28-2001||03:42 PM||||You might want to put on a search of TerryK's posts. A couple months ago he listed some other direct bolt on axels. Olds Ciera I think.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||06-28-2001||03:55 PM||||Paul,<BR> It would be the smaller of the two. some places ask for size of axle nut, it is 30mm. Some places ask LD or HD brakes, it is LD. And some ask for the thread size, it is .785" . Just three different ways of determining which outter joint they have. <P>I've only seen one type of mounting brackets on these transmissions, so I'll say that brackets from <B>any</B> 4t60 will work. They also work on a 4t60e. The stock Fiero rubber mounts get reused. The rear mounting holes in the cradle need to be slotted toward the center of the carabout 1.5", the front set of holes are already positioned perfectly.<BR>Starting in around '90, they switched to a one piece bracket/mount on the front side. This will work in place of the metal bracket and rubber mount, but it's a little mushy. Not as solid as the older design.<BR> I've done a few of these swaps, but never with a 2.8. I suppose you'd need to fab a bracket to bolt the RH 'leg' of the trans to the block, or get one from a 2.8/4spd car. The shift linkage always seems to be the worst part of the swap. The shift arm needs to be flipped and it may interfere with the exhaust crossover pipe. The length of the little arm also needs to be adjusted to match the ratio of the Fiero shifter so that the detents line up. And the cable mounting bracket needs to be modified accordingly.<P>Jon||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||06-28-2001||03:58 PM||||The Cierra axles will not fit because they are not available with the small outter joints. They are the correct length, but you need to pull the outters off and put manual Fiero outters on them. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||SteveJ||06-28-2001||04:16 PM||||Here's a couple of those TerryK links:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006479.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006479.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006837.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006837.html</A> <P>and yes he is swapping the outers. I guess what I was remembering was he had a way to do it without cutting the axel.||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||06-28-2001||04:21 PM||||Well, actually like all A cars (The Ciera is one) they came in two flavors, 100mm hubs for stock suspension and brakes and 115mm hubs for JAx HD brakes and F41, etc suspension.<P>The problem is I've *never* seen a 440T4 or 4T60 with the 100mm hubs. They've always been 115mm hub. That doesn't mean they don't exist, but I've been all over A cars for years and that's something I've never seen.<P>Next, the axles and inner CV's used with 100mm hubs are generally smaller than the 115mm.<P>Sooooo, you will need the axles for the 4 spd auto and swap in the manual trans Fiero outer CV's so the splines will fit the 100mm hubs. <P>||||reg||1
